WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.066 --> 00:00:01.401
A powerful telescope

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:03.503
has captured unprecedented

00:00:03.503 --> 00:00:05.839
and really beautiful images of the sun's

00:00:05.839 --> 00:00:07.941
surface, revealing sunspots

00:00:07.941 --> 00:00:09.809
and other features in new detail.

00:00:09.809 --> 00:00:10.910
Also, Saturn's

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:12.078
iconic rings

00:00:12.078 --> 00:00:13.013
may not be visible

00:00:13.013 --> 00:00:14.047
from Earth in the future.

00:00:14.047 --> 00:00:15.515
Data analysis of NASA's

00:00:15.515 --> 00:00:17.550
13 year Cassini mission

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:18.251
is unveiling

00:00:18.251 --> 00:00:19.753
new insight into how long

00:00:19.753 --> 00:00:21.354
those rings have been around

00:00:21.354 --> 00:00:23.490
and when potentially

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:24.557
they'll vanish from sight.

00:00:24.557 --> 00:00:25.125
Let's bring in now

00:00:25.125 --> 00:00:26.393
president of Explore

00:00:26.393 --> 00:00:29.029
Mars Inc., Janet Ivey, to discuss

00:00:29.329 --> 00:00:31.564
that and more. Let's talk first.

00:00:31.564 --> 00:00:33.900
Good morning about the sun

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:36.169
and these amazing images.

00:00:36.169 --> 00:00:38.271
We're seeing some dark spots here.

00:00:38.304 --> 00:00:39.572
We're seeing what I'm learning

00:00:39.572 --> 00:00:41.007
are some bridges.

00:00:41.007 --> 00:00:42.242
What do we see

00:00:42.776 --> 00:00:44.210
looks like possible?

00:00:45.145 --> 00:00:46.780
No, it's amazing

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:48.214
because what the new

00:00:48.214 --> 00:00:49.916
telescope has captured

00:00:49.916 --> 00:00:52.519
are these beautiful dark sun spots.

00:00:52.752 --> 00:00:55.355
And we are in a time of solar maximum.

00:00:55.488 --> 00:00:57.257
The sun goes through

00:00:57.257 --> 00:00:58.825
an 11 year solar cycle.

00:00:58.825 --> 00:01:01.027
So solar minimum to solar maximum.

00:01:01.194 --> 00:01:02.395
We're in solar maximum.

00:01:02.395 --> 00:01:04.431
What these sunspots are revealing,

00:01:04.431 --> 00:01:05.131
they're seeing this.

00:01:05.131 --> 00:01:05.965
It's weird

00:01:05.965 --> 00:01:08.268
because it's like plasma pushing upwards

00:01:08.435 --> 00:01:11.604
and then cooler plasma pushing downwards.

00:01:11.871 --> 00:01:12.972
It's just beautiful.

00:01:12.972 --> 00:01:14.741
They kind of look like honeycomb

00:01:14.741 --> 00:01:16.276
or flames coming out.

00:01:16.276 --> 00:01:17.510
These are regions

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:19.245
that are huge magnetic regions

00:01:19.245 --> 00:01:21.347
that solar flares can occur

00:01:21.514 --> 00:01:23.817
and then that can cause a lot of problems

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:26.219
here on Earth when they happen.

00:01:26.219 --> 00:01:28.088
But as just an image

00:01:28.088 --> 00:01:29.689
and beauty, it's beautiful.

00:01:29.689 --> 00:01:31.157
And what's really weird

00:01:31.157 --> 00:01:32.025
is that, Victor,

00:01:32.025 --> 00:01:33.326
some of those sunspots

00:01:33.326 --> 00:01:35.028
are three city blocks long.

00:01:35.028 --> 00:01:36.796
And then the one really big one

00:01:36.796 --> 00:01:38.131
you might be looking at

00:01:38.131 --> 00:01:39.966
is at least as big

00:01:39.966 --> 00:01:41.634
as the entire planet of Earth,

00:01:41.634 --> 00:01:42.902
because here's a little sun fact.

00:01:42.902 --> 00:01:44.871
Everybody needs to know over

00:01:44.871 --> 00:01:46.873
1 million suns could fit.

00:01:46.873 --> 00:01:48.441
I mean, 1 million Earths

00:01:48.441 --> 00:01:49.943
could fit inside the sun.

00:01:49.943 --> 00:01:51.878
Amazing. Can't even fathom.

00:01:51.878 --> 00:01:53.513
These images are really beautiful.

00:01:53.513 --> 00:01:55.081
And we're both lovers of art.

00:01:55.081 --> 00:01:55.982
And I love the color

00:01:55.982 --> 00:01:57.550
yellow or chartreuse.

00:01:57.550 --> 00:01:58.551
I have it in my living room.

00:01:58.551 --> 00:01:59.853
I will have to just blow that up.

00:01:59.853 --> 00:02:00.420
And just

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:01.688
because it looks like acrylic art

00:02:01.688 --> 00:02:04.791
or pasta as as a like noodles.

00:02:04.791 --> 00:02:05.758
Right. We're back on the

00:02:06.793 --> 00:02:07.060
of

00:02:08.895 --> 00:02:10.563
Beautiful.

00:02:10.563 --> 00:02:11.464
Go ahead.

00:02:12.799 --> 00:02:15.301
Oh, Saturn's rings are amazing.

00:02:15.301 --> 00:02:16.970
And this is new information.

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:17.337
Remember,

00:02:17.337 --> 00:02:19.239
Cassini crash dived

00:02:19.239 --> 00:02:21.141
at the end of its mission

00:02:21.141 --> 00:02:22.242
and what it was doing,

00:02:22.242 --> 00:02:23.176
it would sort of dove

00:02:23.176 --> 00:02:25.044
in between those rings.

00:02:25.044 --> 00:02:25.845
And scientists

00:02:25.845 --> 00:02:27.814
noticed this interesting phenomena

00:02:27.814 --> 00:02:29.582
called ring reign.

00:02:29.582 --> 00:02:31.417
And so there for a long time,

00:02:31.417 --> 00:02:32.852
we thought maybe something crashed

00:02:32.852 --> 00:02:33.786
into Saturn.

00:02:33.786 --> 00:02:35.221
That material dispersed.

00:02:35.221 --> 00:02:35.889
And then,

00:02:35.889 --> 00:02:36.256
you know,

00:02:36.256 --> 00:02:38.358
the gravitational pull of Saturn

00:02:38.358 --> 00:02:39.225
pulled those rings

00:02:39.225 --> 00:02:41.261
and they began to spin around it.

00:02:41.261 --> 00:02:43.263
Now, there may be some other evidence

00:02:43.263 --> 00:02:45.298
that maybe there was once a moon

00:02:45.298 --> 00:02:45.932
and maybe

00:02:45.932 --> 00:02:47.600
the gravitational instability

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:50.970
call caused that kind of debris field.

00:02:51.704 --> 00:02:52.672
But remember,

00:02:52.672 --> 00:02:53.673
a lot of Saturn's

00:02:53.673 --> 00:02:54.707
what's in Saturn's

00:02:54.707 --> 00:02:56.209
rings are things made of comets,

00:02:56.209 --> 00:02:58.411
asteroids, ice, rock and dust.

00:02:58.411 --> 00:02:59.412
But what they're seeing

00:02:59.412 --> 00:03:00.647
is every time

00:03:00.647 --> 00:03:03.016
those rings that never stop spinning

00:03:03.183 --> 00:03:03.917
get hit with

00:03:03.917 --> 00:03:05.885
something out there in our solar system,

00:03:05.885 --> 00:03:08.354
it's sort of sheering off

00:03:08.354 --> 00:03:09.789
some of that

00:03:09.789 --> 00:03:10.623
some of that ice

00:03:10.623 --> 00:03:11.758
rock and debris in there.

00:03:11.758 --> 00:03:12.859
So scientists

00:03:12.859 --> 00:03:15.662
theorize that billions of years from now

00:03:15.895 --> 00:03:17.397
that they may disappear

00:03:17.397 --> 00:03:18.865
So I hope they stick around

00:03:18.865 --> 00:03:20.333
for a long, long time.

00:03:20.333 --> 00:03:21.334
So we have a little time,

00:03:21.334 --> 00:03:22.635
billions of years from now.

00:03:22.635 --> 00:03:24.204
They won't disappear on Monday.

00:03:24.204 --> 00:03:24.804
We've got

00:03:24.804 --> 00:03:26.906
we're going to go a while longer. Okay.

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:28.107
So let's turn now

00:03:28.107 --> 00:03:31.511
to NASA's Mars rover Perseverance.

00:03:31.711 --> 00:03:33.613
Friends call in Percy.

00:03:33.613 --> 00:03:35.882
We're getting some new video

00:03:36.382 --> 00:03:39.919
looking inside the belly of a crater.

00:03:40.753 --> 00:03:41.521
What are we seeing

00:03:44.857 --> 00:03:46.326
so Belva Crater is

00:03:46.326 --> 00:03:48.394
kind of like a crater in a crater.

00:03:48.394 --> 00:03:49.629
Remember, perseverance

00:03:49.629 --> 00:03:50.863
landed in a place called

00:03:50.863 --> 00:03:51.931
Jezero Crater

00:03:51.931 --> 00:03:54.834
and its main mission is looking for life.

00:03:55.001 --> 00:03:57.370
But this vista really kind of

00:03:57.370 --> 00:03:59.105
has some scientists puzzling,

00:03:59.105 --> 00:04:00.240
like the way

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:02.208
that the rock is sort of scattered

00:04:02.208 --> 00:04:03.276
and some of the mosaic

00:04:03.276 --> 00:04:05.578
and the way the sand structures are

00:04:05.578 --> 00:04:06.412
really suggest

00:04:06.412 --> 00:04:07.280
that there was faster

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:09.415
flowing water through this region.

00:04:09.415 --> 00:04:12.452
And as Danny Rocha and Ted Lasso says,

00:04:12.452 --> 00:04:13.920
football is life.

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:15.521
Well, water means life.

00:04:15.521 --> 00:04:18.992
So that is the quest of perseverance,

00:04:18.992 --> 00:04:21.361
is that we're looking for those by

00:04:22.795 --> 00:04:24.030
biosignatures.

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:25.298
We're looking for fossilized

00:04:25.298 --> 00:04:26.933
life, microbial life.

00:04:26.933 --> 00:04:28.201
But what you're seeing

00:04:28.201 --> 00:04:30.303
is just really this amazing crater

00:04:30.303 --> 00:04:31.404
within a crater

00:04:31.404 --> 00:04:32.372
that we know

00:04:32.372 --> 00:04:33.373
that flowing water

00:04:33.373 --> 00:04:35.375
existed at one point in history.

00:04:35.375 --> 00:04:37.477
More to come, I'm sure, as scientists

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:39.145
keep reviewing that.

00:04:39.145 --> 00:04:39.612
All right,

00:04:39.612 --> 00:04:40.780
Janet Ivy,

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:42.048
always good to have you

00:04:42.048 --> 00:04:42.949
on a Sunday morning.

00:04:42.949 --> 00:04:43.950
Thank you so much.

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:44.951
Always learn something new.

00:04:44.951 --> 00:04:46.352
I didn't know Saturn.

00:04:46.352 --> 00:04:48.087
The rings are made up of asteroids

00:04:48.087 --> 00:04:48.921
and debris and stuff.

00:04:48.921 --> 00:04:50.623
Yeah, and Super B is a million

00:04:50.623 --> 00:04:52.558
Earths into the sun. Had no idea.

00:04:52.558 --> 00:04:53.293
All right. Thank you, Janet.

